/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  inorganicLadders01_goldberg -- the ionisation ladders of phosphoric, pyrophosphoric,
  arsenic and sulfurous acids and of hydrazine at 25 C: seven beakers, each made up at a
  half-neutralisation point of one step, the pH a RESULT of the charge balance, read
  against the evaluated pK's of Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31
  (2002) 231, Section 8 (p. 359-360).

  WHAT THIS CASE IS.  A STRUCTURAL ILLUSTRATION of the C373 phase 2 records (pyrophosphate,
  arsenate, sulfite and hydrazine ladders, written by bin/curate/species_c373.py from
  data/curation/batches/species_c373).  It is NOT validated against a measured pH: no work
  in hand prints a pH for these solutions, so there are no `anchor` rows.  The phosphate
  beaker runs on the catalogue's earlier PHREEQC records (pK2 7.207 there, 7.198 in
  Goldberg), which this campaign compared and did not change.  `pH solve;` closes the
  charge balance; `totalsBasis stoichiometric` makes the sodium a titrant (a neutral NaOH
  feed) and the chloride of the hydrazine beaker the counter-ion of hydrazinium chloride.

  WHAT TO LOOK FOR.  (this run's numbers, Davies, 25 C)
   1. For two neighbouring species at equal molality the Davies pH is pK + log10(gamma_B /
      gamma_A) exactly: arsenate (H2AsO4-/HAsO4--, 0.025 each, I = 0.100) pH 6.728 =
      7.05 - 0.322 (log 0.3725/0.7812); sulfite (HSO3-/SO3--) 6.850 = 7.172 - 0.322;
      hydrazinium (N2H5+/N2H4, I = 0.100) 8.127 = 8.02 + 0.107, the neutral base having
      gamma 1.  The difference to the printed pK is the activity-coefficient ratio, not an
      error of the constant.
   2. Phosphate, equimolar H2PO4-/HPO4-- (0.05 each, I = 0.200): pH 6.826 = 7.207 - 0.38.
   3. Pyrophosphate: the first two steps overlap (pK 0.83 and 2.26) and the three beakers
      at 1.5, 2.5 and 3.5 equivalents of sodium give pH 2.230, 6.068 and 8.510 against pK2,
      pK3, pK4 = 2.26, 6.72, 9.46: the gap grows with the charge of the pair (1.5 eq: H3P2O7-
      0.0164 against H2P2O7-- 0.0331, the steps overlap; 3.5 eq: HP2O7--- 0.0248 and P2O7----
      0.0251, gamma 0.059 and 0.0066, a factor of 9 between the two, so -0.95 pH).  Beyond
      I ~ 0.4 the Davies window is passed, which is why the series stops at 3.5 equivalents.
   4. No ion pairing with sodium for any of these anions: the catalogue holds none, so the
      distribution is complete only up to the reactions somebody wrote down.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    { name phosphate_equimolar; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { PO4 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.15 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H3PO4aq H2PO4 HPO4 PO4 ); output { file phosphate_equimolar.csv; } }
    { name pyrophosphate_1p5eq; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H4P2O7 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.075 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H4P2O7 H3P2O7 H2P2O7 HP2O7 P2O7 ); output { file pyrophosphate_1p5eq.csv; } }
    { name pyrophosphate_2p5eq; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H4P2O7 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.125 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H4P2O7 H3P2O7 H2P2O7 HP2O7 P2O7 ); output { file pyrophosphate_2p5eq.csv; } }
    { name pyrophosphate_3p5eq; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H4P2O7 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.175 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H4P2O7 H3P2O7 H2P2O7 HP2O7 P2O7 ); output { file pyrophosphate_3p5eq.csv; } }
    { name arsenate_1p5eq; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H3AsO4 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.075 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H3AsO4 H2AsO4 HAsO4 AsO4 ); output { file arsenate_1p5eq.csv; } }
    { name sulfite_1p5eq; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H2SO3 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.075 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2SO3 Bisulfite Sulfite ); output { file sulfite_1p5eq.csv; } }
    { name hydrazine_half; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { N2H6 0.05 mol/kg; Cl 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.075 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( N2H6 N2H5 Hydrazine ); output { file hydrazine_half.csv; } }
);
